Fallow is a codebase analyzer written in Rust for TypeScript and JavaScript projects, created by Bart Waardenburg. I contribute to it, and my pull requests are where most of this understanding came from. It finds unused files, dead exports, unlisted dependencies, code duplication, circular dependencies, and complexity hotspots. It's a Rust alternative to Knip.By the project's own benchmarks it runs 6 to 46 times faster than Knip on real repositories, with 4 to 11 times less memory. These are the internals behind those numbers.No TypeScript CompilerThe biggest performance decision was skipping the TypeScript compiler. Knip runs through tsc to build a type-checked AST. This is thorough but expensive: you pay for type checking, declaration merging, and module resolution that a dead-code detector doesn't need.Fallow uses Oxc, a Rust-native parser for JavaScript and TypeScript. Oxc parses syntax into an AST and provides scope-aware binding analysis through oxc_semantic, but it doesn't do type checking. For dead code detection, you need to know "what does this file export?" and "what does this file import?" Both are syntactic questions.The cost is that Fallow can't detect unused type-narrowing exports or exports that are only used via type inference. These are rare enough that the 6-46x speedup is worth it, and the project's creator made that call deliberately.Cache-Friendly Edge StorageThe module graph is the core data structure. Each file is a ModuleNode, and edges represent imports between files. The naive approach is storing edges as Vec<Edge> per node (adjacency list). This scatters edge data across the heap, killing cache performance during traversal.Fallow stores all edges in a single contiguous Vec<Edge>, with each module node holding a Range<usize> into that array:pub struct ModuleNode {
    pub file_id: FileId,
    pub path: PathBuf,
    pub edge_range: Range<usize>,  // range into flat edges array
    pub exports: Vec<ExportSymbol>,
    pub re_exports: Vec<ReExportEdge>,
    pub is_entry_point: bool,
    pub is_reachable: bool,
    // ...
}When you traverse a module's imports, you're iterating over a contiguous slice of memory, which the CPU prefetcher loves. It's the same pattern game engines use for entity-component systems: structure of arrays instead of array of structures.The project also enforces compile-time size assertions to prevent accidental bloat:#[cfg(target_pointer_width = "64")]
const _: () = assert!(std::mem::size_of::<ModuleNode>() == 104);
const _: () = assert!(std::mem::size_of::<SymbolReference>() == 16);If someone adds a field that bumps the size, the build fails, so memory regressions get caught before they show up as slowdowns.Suffix Array Clone DetectionCode duplication detection usually involves pairwise comparison of every pair of code blocks for similarity. That's O(n^2) and falls apart on large codebases.Fallow uses suffix arrays instead:Tokenize each source file into a sequence of abstract tokens (identifiers normalized, literals replaced with type markers)Concatenate all token sequences with sentinel separators between filesBuild a suffix array over the concatenated sequence using O(N log N) prefix-doubling with radix sortCompute the LCP array (Longest Common Prefix) to find maximal repeated subsequencesExtract clone families from LCP runs that span different filesThis finds every duplicated block across the codebase in a single pass, with no pairwise comparison. Building the suffix array is the expensive step, at O(N log N) where N is the total token count, compared with O(files^2) for pairwise matching.On Next.js with 20,000 files, duplication detection completes in about 3 seconds. jscpd (the standard JavaScript duplicate detector) takes 24 seconds on the same codebase.Rayon Parallelism with Incremental CachingParsing is embarrassingly parallel, since each file is independent. Fallow uses Rayon to parse files across all available CPU cores. On a laptop with a handful of cores, that alone cuts parsing time several-fold.But cold parsing is still the bottleneck for large projects. Fallow's caching layer stores parsed results keyed by file content hash. On subsequent runs, only modified files get re-parsed. This turns a 1.5-second cold analysis into a ~200ms warm analysis, because most files don't change between runs.Because the cache key is a content hash, renaming a file without changing its content doesn't invalidate the cache, and two files with identical content share a cache entry.84 Framework Plugins, Zero ConfigFallow ships with 84 plugins that detect framework-specific patterns (Next.js page routes, Storybook stories, Vitest test files, Tailwind configs, etc.) and mark the relevant files as entry points. Without this, a dead-code detector would flag your pages/index.tsx as unused because nothing explicitly imports it.The plugins are auto-detected from package.json dependencies, with no configuration. If you have next in your dependencies, Fallow knows that files matching app/**/page.tsx are entry points, which is how it gets away with zero config on real projects.BenchmarksBenchmarked on Apple M5, 32GB RAM, median of 5 runs:ProjectFilesFallowKnip v5SpeedupFallow RSSKnip RSSzod17419ms639ms34x21 MB250 MBfastify28624ms1.13s46x27 MB289 MBvue/core52263ms702ms11x34 MB271 MBsvelte3,337325ms1.93s6x67 MB460 MBnext.js20,4161.48s(crashes)--194 MB--Knip v5 can't analyze Next.js at all (exits with errors). Fallow handles it in under 2 seconds using less memory than Knip uses on a project 1/10th the size.The speed comes from all of these decisions together: skipping the type checker, keeping data structures cache-friendly, finding duplication with suffix arrays, and parsing in parallel with an incremental cache. Each layer of the pipeline picks the faster option. Contributing to a codebase with this much performance engineering has been one of the best ways I've found to learn fast Rust.

Pyscn is a code quality analyzer for Python built by DaisukeYoda at ludo-technologies. It finds dead code, code clones, coupling issues, and complexity hotspots. It's written in Go, uses tree-sitter for parsing, and the project reports processing over 100,000 lines per second.I contribute to it (my pull requests, including the one that made dependency analysis scale), and a few of its design decisions are interesting enough to write up: how it detects dead code through control flow analysis, how it finds duplicated code across clone types 1-4 with LSH acceleration, and how it plugs into AI coding assistants via MCP.Why Go + tree-sitterPython has good AST modules (ast, astroid), but they're slow on large codebases, and an analyzer written in Python is bottlenecked by the interpreter. Go gives native performance, and tree-sitter provides a mature Python parser so nobody has to write a custom grammar.tree-sitter is a parser generator used by editors like Neovim, Helix, and Zed. Its Go binding (go-tree-sitter) lets you parse Python source files into concrete syntax trees, then walk those trees at Go speed.The catch is that tree-sitter produces CSTs (concrete syntax trees), so you get every token including whitespace and punctuation. That means more node types to handle. It also means you can reconstruct exact source locations, which is useful for reporting the lines of dead code.Dead Code via Control Flow GraphsMost dead code detectors look for unused functions or unreachable imports. Pyscn also builds a Control Flow Graph for each function and finds code that no execution path can reach.CFG construction goes like this:Parse the function body with tree-sitterCreate nodes for each statement or expressionAdd edges for sequential flow and branching (if/elif/else, try/except, loops)Mark exit points (return, raise, break, continue)Walk from entry to find all reachable nodesReport any node that wasn't reachedExhaustive branches are the interesting case. If every branch of an if/elif/else chain ends with a return or raise, then code after the chain is unreachable:def process(status):
    if status == "active":
        return handle_active()
    elif status == "pending":
        return handle_pending()
    else:
        raise ValueError(f"Unknown status: {status}")

    # This code is dead — all branches above exit the function
    log_completion()  # pyscn catches thisSimple detectors miss this because they don't track control flow through branches. Pyscn's CFG analysis handles nested branches, early returns inside loops, and try/except/finally blocks.Clone Detection: Types 1-4Pyscn classifies duplicated code into four types:TypeDefinitionExampleType 1Identical except whitespace/commentsCopy-pasted verbatimType 2Identical structure, different identifiers/literalsRenamed variablesType 3Similar structure with small modificationsAdded/removed a lineType 4Same logic, different syntaxList comp vs. for loopTypes 1 and 2 are detected by normalizing the AST (stripping identifiers, replacing literals with type markers) and hashing the result. Identical hashes mean identical structure.Type 3 detection applies a similarity threshold to the normalized token sequences, and that gets expensive: naive pairwise comparison is O(n^2) in the number of code blocks.Pyscn uses Locality-Sensitive Hashing (LSH) to cut that down. It hashes each block into multiple buckets using MinHash signatures, and blocks that land in the same bucket are likely similar. Only blocks that share a bucket get compared directly, which shrinks the comparison space a lot.Type 4 is the hardest to catch, since the syntax differs. Pyscn handles it with semantic normalization: both [x for x in items if x > 0] and the equivalent for loop get normalized to a similar token sequence before comparison.MCP IntegrationPyscn ships as a CLI tool and as an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server. The MCP server exposes the same analysis to AI coding assistants like Claude Code, Cursor, and ChatGPT.So you can ask your assistant "find duplicate code in this directory" and it calls pyscn, gets structured analysis results back, and can reason about them and suggest refactors.# Claude Code setup
claude mcp add pyscn-mcp uvx -- pyscn-mcpThe MCP server wraps the same Go binary the CLI uses, so there's one analysis engine and the results are identical either way. The Go microservice does the analysis, and a thin Python wrapper (pyscn on PyPI) handles distribution and MCP transport.PerformanceThe 100K+ lines/sec figure comes from Go's concurrency model and tree-sitter's parsing speed. File parsing runs concurrently across goroutines, and since each file is independent the analysis parallelizes without much effort. tree-sitter parsers are generated C code called through Go's FFI, so the parsing runs at native speed.

Obelisk is the AI marketing platform we build at Entropy Labs. It runs more than fifteen specialist agents: brand voice, SEO, email, paid ads, strategy and so on, all behind one API.Most of the work ended up in what wraps each model call: which model to use, what to do when the conversation gets long, how to stop an agent calling the same tool forever, when to ask a human before spending money, and what to do when something fails. Early on, each agent handled those things its own way, which didn't last long.We pulled every one of those concerns into middleware, small separate layers that run around every model and tool call and that each agent opts in or out of. LangChain's agent middleware gave us the hooks. Below are the decisions that mattered. Our thresholds and routing rules are left out.One concern per layerEach middleware does one thing: pick the model, trim the context, cap the calls, gate the dangerous tools, or recover from errors. An agent declares which ones it wants. The SEO agent, which uses a handful of tools, doesn't need tool filtering. The strategy agent, which can reach dozens of tools, does.Order matters a lot. Model selection has to run first, so every later layer knows which model it's dealing with. Context trimming has to run before the call-limit check, or a long conversation burns its budget on retries. Approval gates have to sit close to the tool call, so nothing downstream can reroute around them. We found most of those orderings by getting them wrong.Picking the model per requestObelisk supports several model providers, and the model is chosen when a request arrives. The same agent can run on one provider for one customer and on another for a customer with a different contract or their own keys.That was painful to build, because every provider streams, counts tokens and reports errors a little differently. In return we can use cheap models for cheap jobs like summarizing, accept customer-supplied keys, and fall back when one provider has a bad afternoon.Summarizing long conversationsLong sessions with tool calls and research results fill context windows quickly. When a conversation gets close to a threshold, a summarization layer compresses the older messages with a cheap model and keeps the recent ones as they are.The threshold is set well below the smallest context window we support, so switching models mid-conversation never overflows. Every summarization is also recorded, with when it fired and how much it compressed. When an agent "forgets" something, that record is the first thing we check.Filtering tools per requestObelisk has a lot of tools. Handing all of them to every call is a bad idea: the model spends tokens reading descriptions it won't use, and a longer menu means more wrong picks. For agents with large toolsets, a cheap classifier picks a small relevant subset per request, and a few tools, like knowledge-base search, are always included.After that went in, the logs showed fewer irrelevant tool calls and a lot less prompt spent on tool descriptions.Approval before sending or spendingAnything that sends, publishes or spends goes through an approval gate. The agent proposes the call, the graph pauses, and the person sees what's about to happen, including a spend warning when there's money involved. They can approve it, edit the arguments, or reject it.HumanInTheLoopMiddleware(
    interrupt_on={
        "send_campaign_email": {"allowed_decisions": ["approve", "edit", "reject"]},
        "publish_ad": {"allowed_decisions": ["approve", "reject"]},
        "search_knowledge_base": False,
    },
)This needs a checkpointer, because the conversation has to survive the pause, sometimes for hours. More than anything else in the system, this gate is what made people comfortable letting agents touch real accounts.RetriesThe default instinct with errors is "retry three times and hope". That's fine for a timeout. For a malformed email it means sending it malformed three times.So errors get classified first. Timeouts and rate limits get retried with backoff; validation and auth failures don't. When retries run out, the agent gets the error as a message and decides what to do (try something else, ask the user, say it failed), and the conversation carries on.@wrap_tool_call
def retry_transient(request, handler):
    for attempt in range(MAX_ATTEMPTS):
        try:
            return handler(request)
        except TransientError:
            if attempt == MAX_ATTEMPTS - 1:
                raise
            sleep(backoff(attempt))Tool inputs are validated against their schemas before the call, so a bad argument fails before any side effect starts.Splitting the general agentThe first version had one general agent with a very long system prompt that tried to cover every job. It wrote ad copy when asked for analytics and started research when someone wanted a quick answer.Splitting it into specialists fixed most of that. Each agent has a short prompt, its own tools and its own output style, and a bug in one doesn't break the others. We route to agents directly from the product, since the UI already knows which kind of conversation it's in, so there's no agent picking agents. That's simpler to debug and saves a model call whose only job is deciding what the next model call should be.Middleware first, logs from day oneWe bolted the middleware onto working agents over several months. Building it first and adding agents on top would have saved a lot of refactoring.Structured events should have been there from the start too. Debugging a many-agent system with print statements does not scale, and request IDs on every event came later than they should have. There's a post on structured logging about what we do now.An orchestrator agent is tempting, and I'd still resist it. Direct routing plus good middleware is more predictable than a model deciding which model to call.

In Polaris, the POS system I built for retail shops, the invoice page has a product search bar. A cashier types a product name, results appear, and they click to add it to the invoice.The first version fired an API call on every keystroke. On a shop with 500 products, search took 3-4 seconds, because each request hit the database, serialized the response and traveled back over the network. Cashiers waited on every search, which hurt during rush hours.Loading the catalog on mountThe product catalog for a typical Polaris shop is 200-800 products, maybe 50KB of JSON. There's no reason to fetch it on every search when you can load it once.<script setup>
import { ref, onMounted } from 'vue'
import { useProductStore } from '@/stores/product'

const productStore = useProductStore()
const searchQuery = ref('')
const searchResults = ref([])

onMounted(async () => {
  await productStore.fetchProducts()
})

function searchProducts() {
  if (!searchQuery.value.trim()) {
    searchResults.value = []
    return
  }
  const query = searchQuery.value.toLowerCase()
  searchResults.value = productStore.products.filter(p =>
    p.name.toLowerCase().includes(query) ||
    p.sku?.toLowerCase().includes(query)
  )
}
</script>Search went from 3-4 seconds to effectively instant. Fetching the catalog added maybe 200ms to the initial page load, but that happens once while the page is rendering anyway, and the cashier doesn't notice.When this breaks downThis pattern has a ceiling. It works for Polaris because:The catalog size is bounded. Retail shops have hundreds of products. If you're building an e-commerce platform with 50,000 SKUs, preloading everything is not an option.The data changes slowly. Products don't update mid-transaction. If your data changes every few seconds, you'll serve stale results.The search is simple string matching on name and SKU. If you need fuzzy matching, relevance scoring or faceted search, you need a server-side search engine.Options for bigger catalogsFor larger catalogs, the progression is:Debounced server search. Wait 300ms after the last keystroke, then fire one API call, which stops the per-keystroke storm.Server-side search with an index, such as PostgreSQL trigram indexes or Elasticsearch. You need this once you're past a few thousand items.Virtual scrolling for results. If you're showing 10,000 results in a dropdown, render only what's visible; libraries like vue-virtual-scroller handle this.Which one you need depends on how much data you have and how fast it changes. For Polaris, preloading 500 products on mount was the simplest fix for a cashier waiting four seconds per search.

Early in building Polaris, I extracted a ConfirmDialog component after using it exactly once. I figured I'd need it everywhere (deletes, password changes, dangerous actions). Classic foresight.Then the second use case needed a text input inside the dialog. The third needed two buttons with custom labels instead of "Confirm/Cancel". The fourth needed async validation before confirming. Within a month, my clean ConfirmDialog had 8 props, 3 slots, and a mode prop that changed how it behaved.I'd have been better off copy-pasting the original 20 lines and tweaking each one independently.The 3-Usage-Sites RuleI now wait until three call sites would all benefit from the same change before extracting. Looking similar doesn't count.Two similar components might diverge tomorrow. Three that stay identical for a few weeks probably share real structure worth abstracting.The Price DisplayThe price display was worth extracting. In Polaris, prices show up in invoices, product lists, reports, and receipts. Every one of them needs the same formatting: locale-aware number formatting with the currency symbol from the shop's settings.<script setup lang="ts">
import { computed } from 'vue'
import { useShopStore } from '@/stores/shop'

const props = defineProps<{
  amount: number
}>()

const shop = useShopStore()

const formatted = computed(() =>
  new Intl.NumberFormat(shop.locale, {
    style: 'currency',
    currency: shop.currency,
  }).format(props.amount)
)
</script>

<template>
  <span>{{ formatted }}</span>
</template>It has one prop and no slots or modes, and every call site wants the same thing: show this number as a price. When I changed the formatting logic to handle zero-decimal currencies, every price picked up the fix.The test I use is whether a change here would fix a real inconsistency across the app. If so, extract. If each usage is going to diverge, copy-paste and let them evolve separately.Signals That You Extracted Too EarlyThe component has a mode or variant prop that changes its behavior, beyond styling.You're adding slots to handle "special cases" in specific call sites.You find yourself passing callbacks through 3 layers of props to handle one caller's edge case.The component file is growing faster than the features that use it.Signals That You Should ExtractYou fixed a bug in one place and realized you need to fix the identical bug in 2 other files.Three or more call sites pass the same props in the same pattern.A formatting rule (dates, currencies, statuses) should be consistent but you've implemented it slightly differently in each place.The aim is to split code along the lines where it changes. Sometimes that means a shared component, and sometimes three similar-looking blocks that serve different purposes.

When an AI coding assistant tries to help you debug a production issue, it reads your logs. If your logs are scattered console.log calls with inconsistent formatting, it can't do much with them. It doesn't know which log lines belong to the same request, what the timing was, or what the error context means.Evlog is a structured logging library by Hugo Richard, designed around the "wide event" pattern: one structured event per request, with all the context attached. I've been using it in my projects, and it's particularly useful when you're debugging with AI tools, because the log output is machine-readable by design.The Problem with console.logA typical Node.js app logs like this:[INFO] Processing order 12345
[INFO] User: john@example.com
[DEBUG] Fetching inventory for SKU-789
[WARN] Inventory low: 3 remaining
[INFO] Order processed in 847ms
[ERROR] Failed to send confirmation emailThat's six log lines for one request. To understand what happened you have to stitch them together by timing and work out which lines belong to which request when there are 50 concurrent users.An AI assistant reading these logs has the same problem, and without explicit correlation it can't infer the causal chain between lines.One Event Per RequestWith evlog you build up one event over the request lifecycle:import { createRequestLogger } from 'evlog'

export default defineEventHandler(async (event) => {
  const log = createRequestLogger(event)

  log.field('user', user.email)
  log.field('orderId', orderId)

  const items = await fetchInventory(skus)
  log.field('inventoryCount', items.length)

  if (items.some(i => i.stock < 5)) {
    log.field('lowStock', true)
  }

  await processOrder(order)

  // Duration is tracked automatically from request start
  // The event emits on request end with all fields attached
})When the request finishes, evlog emits one structured JSON event:{
  "level": "info",
  "message": "POST /api/orders",
  "timestamp": "2026-03-05T10:23:45.123Z",
  "duration": 847,
  "user": "john@example.com",
  "orderId": 12345,
  "inventoryCount": 3,
  "lowStock": true,
  "env": { "service": "api", "environment": "production" }
}Every field is on the same event, and duration is filled in for you. An AI assistant can read one JSON object and see who made the request, what happened, how long it took and what was unusual.Self-Documenting ErrorsFor AI debugging, the most useful part of evlog is its error structure. Where you'd normally write throw new Error("Failed to sync"), you create errors with why and fix fields:throw new EvlogError({
  message: 'Failed to sync repository',
  status: 503,
  why: 'GitHub API rate limit exceeded',
  fix: 'Wait 1 hour or use a different token',
  link: 'https://docs.github.com/en/rest/rate-limit',
  cause: originalError,
})An AI reading this error in a log gets the message, the root cause and a next step it can act on, which is more than it usually has to go on. That's the difference between an AI saying "there's an error on line 47" and "the GitHub sync failed because you've exceeded the rate limit: you can either wait an hour or switch to a different API token."Automatic Context InjectionEvlog's framework integrations (Nuxt, Next.js, Express, Fastify, Hono, SvelteKit) automatically attach request context without manual wiring:request method and path, from the HTTP layerduration, from request start to response endstatus code, from the responseenvironment (service name, deployment version, commit hash, region), from runtime detectionIn Nuxt it's one module entry:// nuxt.config.ts
export default defineNuxtConfig({
  modules: ['evlog/nuxt'],
  evlog: {
    env: { service: 'my-app' },
  }
})Every server route then gets a request logger with duration tracking. The module injects it, so there's nothing to import and no middleware to set up.Pasting logs into a modelMost of us now paste logs into a model and ask what went wrong, and the answer is only as good as the logs. With correlation, timing and the error's why and fix on one event, the model has what it needs to reason about the problem. With scattered console.log calls it has to guess how the lines relate, and it often guesses wrong. The same JSON event is easier for me to read, too.I liked it enough to send a couple of small fixes upstream: a docs fix for the Cloudflare Workers setup and one that skips unnecessary Nitro adapter probes.

A university project where I built a garbage bin that knows how full it is and tells you about it. An ultrasonic sensor measures the fill level, an ESP8266 sends the data over WiFi, and you can monitor it from a desktop GUI or an Android app.The HardwareAn Arduino Uno with an HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor measures distance to the garbage surface. A servo motor opens the lid when someone approaches (second ultrasonic sensor detects proximity). The Arduino sends distance readings over serial to an ESP8266, which transmits them over WiFi.// The core measurement is simple — pulse timing to distance conversion
long duration = pulseIn(echoPin, HIGH);
long cm = duration / 29 / 2;The ESP8266 connects to a laptop or cloud endpoint over TCP and forwards the readings.What WorkedThe HC-SR04 measured garbage levels to within 1-2cm in a 22cm bin. The math is basic physics (speed of sound, halved for the round trip).Publishing sensor data to ThingSpeak over MQTT gave us a real-time dashboard with almost no code. For prototyping IoT, it's hard to beat.The Kotlin Android app was overkill but fun. It shows bin status and sends a notification when the bin is full. A web dashboard would have been enough, but piping IoT data all the way to a phone notification was a worthwhile exercise.What I'd ChangePower management was an afterthought. The Arduino and ESP8266 run continuously, which drains batteries fast. A production system would need sleep modes: wake up, take a reading, transmit, sleep. We never implemented this because we had wall power during the demo.The ESP8266 drops WiFi connections silently. Our code had a basic reconnect loop that didn't hold up well. A real deployment would want MQTT with QoS 1 (at-least-once delivery) and offline buffering.We added Azure IoT Hub because it sounded impressive for the project defense, while ThingSpeak was already doing everything we needed. I wouldn't add cloud infrastructure to a project that works fine without it again.Physical ConstraintsMost of the difficulty was physical. Mount the sensor wrong and you measure the bin wall instead of the garbage. The ESP8266 has a weak antenna, so WiFi range is limited, and power was a problem the whole way through. Pure software projects never put these in front of you, and I learned more from them than from the code.Source code: Arduino | ESP8266 | Edge Layer

IntroductionFor a university course I wrote a grayscale image editor in C++ that handles the basic image processing tasks. It was mostly practice in file handling, image manipulation, and efficient data structures in C++.FeaturesThe application supports:Loading and saving grayscale images in PGM format.Applying filters like mean and median filtering.Performing transformations such as flipping, rotating, and resizing.Combining images either side-by-side or top-to-bottom.Adjusting brightness and generating negative images.Core ImplementationThe editor is built around the grayImage struct, which stores pixel data and has a function for each image operation. Here’s how pixels are set and read:unsigned short setPixel(unsigned short value, int r, int c) {
    if (r >= Rows || c >= Cols || r < 0 || c < 0) {
        return -1;
    }
    Image[r][c] = value;
    return value;
}

int getPixel(int r, int c) {
    if (r >= Rows || c >= Cols || r < 0 || c < 0) {
        return -1;
    }
    return Image[r][c];
}Loading and Saving ImagesThe editor reads and writes images in PGM format. The load() and Save() functions handle file I/O:int load(string File_Name) {
    ifstream Input(File_Name.c_str());
    if (!Input) {
        return 1;
    }
    string MagicNumber, comment;
    int columns, rows, MaxValue, currentValue;
    getline(Input, MagicNumber);
    getline(Input, comment);
    Input >> columns >> rows >> MaxValue;
    setRows(rows);
    setCols(columns);
    Maximum = MaxValue;

    for (int i = 0; i < Rows; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < Cols; j++) {
            Input >> currentValue;
            Image[i][j] = currentValue;
        }
    }
    Input.close();
    Loaded = true;
    return 0;
}Applying a Negative FilterA negative is one of the simplest transformations: subtract each pixel from the maximum value.void Negative(grayImage& Result) {
    for (int row = 0; row < Rows; row++) {
        for (int column = 0; column < Cols; column++) {
            Result.Image[row][column] = Maximum - Image[row][column];
        }
    }
    Result.Rows = Rows;
    Result.Cols = Cols;
    Result.Maximum = Maximum;
}Future ImprovementsThings I would add next:Adding support for colored images (PPM format).Implementing more advanced filters (e.g., Gaussian blur, edge detection).Providing a GUI using a library like Qt or OpenCV.Looking backWriting it taught me a lot about image data, file I/O, and algorithm optimization, and it was my first step toward more advanced image processing.The full source code is on GitHub.Feedback or suggestions are welcome through the contact page.
